L139: Consistent with our hypothesis, we find that negative distractors induce higher rates of immoral behavior across all models tested, decreasing the MMAP from the baseline ($p<0.05$ for all except Qwen3-4B and GPT-4.1 in high-ambiguity scenarios) and inducing the lowest MMAP among all distractors ($p\ll 0.05$ for all). In most cases positive distractors have the opposite effect, increasing the MMAP from the baseline.
L140: A notable exception is Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, where positive and neutral distractors decrease the MMAP by over 20% and negative distractors by over 30% in low-ambiguity scenarios.
L141: We note that it is somewhat surprising to observe consistent effects in high-ambiguity scenarios, since there is no clearly preferred moral option in such scenarios. For this reason, we theorize that our findings may reflect subtle imbalances in the MoralChoice dataset itself or the distribution of moral rules used for annotation.
L142: Disaggregating results by moral rule, we further find that moral distractors of different valences can shift the preferences of LLMs towards different moral values in specific cases, but do not observe generalizable trends across models (see Appendix cite44†B.2 for a more detailed discussion).
