L153: #### 4.1.1 Ablations
L154: 
L155: Our ablations over model size, instruction-tuning, and reasoning yield the following key findings (see Appendix cite45†B.3 for more detailed results).
L156: ##### Size (cite93†Figure 9 )
L157: 
L158: We do not observe any consistent relationship between model size and sensitivity to moral distractors. In the smallest model evaluated (gemma-3-270m-it), trends are reversed such that negative distractors induce the highest MMAP, showing that the effects of negative distractors are not constant across all models.
L159: ##### Instruction-Tuning (cite94†Figure 10 )
L160: 
L161: We observe that the relationship between instruction-tuning and sensitivity to moral distractors depends on the specific model family. Whereas instruction-tuning for Llama-3.2-3B and Qwen-3-4B increases the effect of moral distractors, instruction-tuning for gemma-3-4b decreases the effect of moral distractors.
L162: ##### Reasoning (cite95†Figure 11 )
L163: 
L164: We observe that enabling reasoning is very effective at mitigating the effects of moral distractors in low-ambiguity scenarios, though there remains a small but statistically insignificant drop in MMAP from negative distractors ($p>0.05$). These results suggest that reasoning may be a promising way to improve the stability of the moral judgements of LLMs.
L165: 
L166: ### 4.2 r/AITA Results
L167: ##### Verdict Distribution
L168: 
L169: The introduction of distractors to r/AITA prompts leads to shifts in verdicts, dependent on distractor valence. Chi-square tests also reveal that verdict distributions show strong statistical dependence on distractor valence for Gemma, Llama, and Qwen models ($p\ll 0.05$), although GPT-4.1 showed no significant dependence ($p>0.05$) (cite96†Table 1 ).
L170: We find that negative distractors have the strongest effects across distractors, consistently causing models to become more disapproving of others’ behavior as reflected in an increased share of “everyone sucks here” verdicts (cite97†Figure 6 ). This effect is most pronounced for Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, where negative distractors increase the share of ESH verdicts by 9.5%.
L171: In addition, for all models except GPT-4.1, positive distractors cause models to become more supportive of the original poster’s behavior, increasing the share of “not the asshole” (NTA) verdicts while decreasing the share of “you’re the asshole” (YTA) verdicts. In general, however, the effects of moral distractors on model responses to the r/AITA dataset are generally less consistent than on model responses to the MoralChoice dataset.
